<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        A dashboard is laid out once on a 12-column grid, but it's seen on very different screens: people's phones,
        computers, and kiosk TVs on the wall. The four buttons at the top of the designer show each one.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Desktop and Tablet">
        <FluentText>
            <strong>Desktop</strong> is where you build the layout. <strong>Tablet</strong> shows the same layout at a
            tablet's width, and you can keep editing there.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Phones">
        <FluentText>
            On a phone, the dashboard becomes one column of widgets. Select the <strong>Phone</strong> button to choose how
            that column looks, with a live phone preview beside the list:
        </FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li>Drag a widget's handle (or use its up and down arrows) to change its place on phones. The desktop layout
                doesn't change.</li>
            <li>Select the eye to hide a widget on phones. It still shows on bigger screens.</li>
            <li><strong>Match desktop</strong> goes back to the desktop order: top to bottom, then left to right.</li>
        </ul>
        <FluentText>You can also hide a widget on phones from its details, with <strong>Show this widget on phones</strong>.</FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Kiosk TVs">
        <FluentText>
            A dashboard reaches a TV through a projection program's Dashboard step. Select the <strong>TV</strong> button to see
            it at 1920 × 1080, scaled down to fit.
        </FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li>Turn on <strong>Fill the screen on kiosk TVs</strong> and the rows stretch so the whole dashboard fits the TV
                with nothing cut off. It's also in the dashboard's details, as <strong>Fill the screen</strong>.</li>
            <li>With it off, rows keep their height, and anything below the bottom of the TV won't be seen, because nobody
                can scroll a kiosk.</li>
            <li><strong>Row height</strong> (Compact, Comfortable or Spacious) in the dashboard's details sets how tall each
                row is everywhere else.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpNote>Changes made in any of these views are kept by the same <strong>Save</strong>, and can be undone like any other change.</HelpNote>
</HelpTab>
